  appends an immutable event: [38;5;153m{id, ts, actor, taskId, type, before, after}[39m. The feed is a query over that log. Read
  state is the watermark plus a small dismissed-ids set.

  [3mWins when[0m you want both triggers and the multi-user door left open. The event log is precisely the piece we identified
  as expensive to retrofit, and it's the same query a server would eventually run, so going multi-user is additive
  rather than a rewrite. It's also the most testable: you can replay a log and assert the exact feed. Complexity:
  moderate — the real cost is discipline (no writing to tasks outside the chokepoint) plus eventual compaction.

  [1mC. Materialized notification records.[0m A dispatcher evaluates rules at mutation time and inserts rows into a
  [38;5;153mnotifications[39m store.

  [3mWins when[0m the read path needs to be dead simple and per-item read state is central — it's what a conventional
  server-side system looks like. [3mBut[0m it creates a second source of truth that drifts: deleted tasks orphan rows, and
  changing a rule doesn't retroactively fix the feed. And it doesn't actually save you the sweep — see below.
  Complexity: moderate.

  [1mThe thing that decides it[0m

  Your two triggers have [1mfundamentally different shapes[0m, and any design that ignores this breaks:

  - [3mStarred task changed[0m is [1mpush[0m: something happens, an event is recorded.
  - [3mDue date arrived[0m is [1mpull[0m: nothing happens. No code runs at 9:00am. The "event" is the clock moving while the app was
    closed.

  So a due-date notification can only ever be [1mcomputed on load[0m by comparing due dates against the interval since your
  last visit. That's the catch-up sweep you already picked, and it's unavoidable. Which means approach C doesn't
  eliminate the sweep — it makes you build the sweep [3mand[0m the dispatcher, and reconcile them. That's the argument that
  pushes me off C.

  [1mMy recommendation: B.[0m One log for recorded changes, one sweep for time-derived triggers, one feed query that merges
  them. It's the only option that serves both triggers without two competing mechanisms, and the log is the piece that's
  painful to add later.

